[SPEAKER_00] on the moon.
[SPEAKER_00] And with NASA's confirmation of ice existing at the lunar South Pole, the critical task
[SPEAKER_00] of finding and mapping where water exists, what form it is in, and where it came from,
[SPEAKER_00] can now begin.
[SPEAKER_00] Leading us on that journey will be NASA's first mobile robotic mission on the moon, known
[SPEAKER_00] as Viper, the volatile investigating polar exploration rover.
[SPEAKER_00] It will be delivered to the nobile region of the South Pole as part of NASA's commercial
[SPEAKER_00] lunar payload services initiative.
[SPEAKER_00] This region sits just outside the western rim of nobile crater, and covers an area of
[SPEAKER_00] 36 square miles.
[SPEAKER_00] As the first ever resource mapping mission on the surface of another celestial body, Viper
[SPEAKER_00] will roam the surface equipped with three science instruments and a drill to detect and analyze
[SPEAKER_00] various lunar soil environments at a range of depths and temperatures.
[SPEAKER_00] The rover will venture into permanently shadowed craters, some of the coldest spots in the
[SPEAKER_00] solar system, where ice reserves have been preserved for billions of years.
[SPEAKER_00] NASA had four critical parameters when choosing a landing site for Viper, available sunlight,
[SPEAKER_00] earth visibility for communications from the moon to the earth, data showing the potential
[SPEAKER_00] presence of water and other resources, and terrain that is well suited for Viper to navigate.
[SPEAKER_00] The area to the west of nobile crater met these conditions.
[SPEAKER_00] Once on the surface, Viper's mission will last 100 days and cover between 10 to 15
[SPEAKER_00] miles.
[SPEAKER_00] And while a baseline traverse through the nobile region has been identified for the rover,
[SPEAKER_00] the scientific discoveries Viper makes along the way will actually influence where the
[SPEAKER_00] mission team sends it next, so its planned route will most likely change.
[SPEAKER_00] During its travels, Viper will visit at least six locations where data suggests ice could
[SPEAKER_00] be found.
[SPEAKER_00] By helping determine the locations of where water and other resources exist, Viper's findings
[SPEAKER_00] will help pave the way for future landing sites under NASA's Artemis program.
